        "content": "Now let me do the detailed editorial review.\n\n---\n\n## Editorial Review: .NET 11 Preview 3 Release Notes\n\n### runtime.md\n\n**File**: runtime.md\n**Heading**: Title `# .NET Runtime in .NET 11 Preview 3`\n**Issue**: Missing `- Release Notes` suffix. All existing component files use `# Component in .NET X Preview Y - Release Notes`.\n**Suggested rewrite**: `# .NET Runtime in .NET 11 Preview 3 - Release Notes`\n\n---\n\n**File**: runtime.md\n**Heading**: `## Runtime Async (V3)`\n**Issue**: \"(V3)\" is invented shorthand — no reader knows what V3 means. The blockquote \"This is a preview feature\" is fine, but the intro paragraph uses promotional language (\"delivers two significant…advances\"). State the changes; let the reader judge significance.\n**Suggested rewrite**:\n```markdown\n## Runtime Async\n\n> This is a preview feature for .NET 11.\n\nPreview 3 adds crossgen2 compilation support and continuation reuse to the runtime-native async infrastructure introduced in Preview 1.\n```\n\n---\n\n**File**: runtime.md\n**Heading**: `### crossgen2 support`\n**Issue**: Heading uses lowercase for a product name. crossgen2 is conventionally capitalized as `Crossgen2` in .NET docs. The section itself is well-written — concrete delta, clear before/after.\n**Suggested rewrite**: `### Crossgen2 support`\n\n---\n\n**File**: runtime.md\n**Heading**: `### Continuation reuse`\n**Issue**: \"The runtime can now save and reuse\" — borderline anthropomorphic. \"A companion JIT optimization skips saving locals that haven't changed\" — good, but \"companion\" is informal. The sentence \"Together these changes lower GC pressure in high-throughput async workloads\" infers an outcome rather than stating a measured delta.\n**Suggested rewrite**:\n```markdown\n### Continuation reuse\n\nAsync methods now save and reuse continuation instances instead of allocating a new one each time they suspend. A related JIT optimization skips re-saving unchanged locals into the reuse