eeds the 3–8 word guideline. Also, `dotnet/dotnet #5916` points at a VMR PR rather than the aspnetcore-specific change, which may confuse readers.\n**Suggested rewrite**: `### Managed Identity in Individual Auth templates`\n\n---\n\n### fsharp.md\n\n**File**: fsharp.md\n**Heading**: Opening line\n**Issue**: \"No significant F# feature changes are highlighted for Preview 3.\" — passive, negative-tone, and unnecessary. The file *does* contain four bug fixes worth noting. Lead with what's there.\n**Suggested rewrite**: `Preview 3 includes four bug fixes for IDE completion, diagnostics, quotations, and symbol API behavior.`\n\n---\n\n**File**: fsharp.md\n**Heading**: Bug fix bullets\n**Issue**: The bold labels (**Completion**, **Diagnostics**, **Quotations**, **Symbol API**) are useful for scanning. However, \"restoring parity with other obsolete member types\" is jargon — parity with what? State the concrete fix.\n**Suggested rewrite**: Change \"restoring parity with other obsolete member types\" to \"so obsolete fields and events appear alongside other obsolete members.\"\n\n---\n\n**File**: fsharp.md\n**Heading**: (structure)\n**Issue**: The \"What's new in F#\" link block appears at the bottom. Existing convention in other component files places this link block near the top, directly after the TOC.\n**Suggested rewrite**: Move the link block to after the H1, before `## Bug fixes`.\n\n---\n\n## Summary: Editorial scores\n\n| Section | Score | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Runtime Async — crossgen2 | 8 | Clear, concrete, well-linked |\n| Runtime Async — continuation reuse | 7 | Good but slightly speculative on outcome |\n| Runtime Async — profiler support | 5 | Niche audience, vague heading |\n| GC regions on macOS | 7 | Good delta, one speculative sentence |\n| SDSU register allocation | 3 | Jargon-heavy, niche, opaque heading |\n| SafeFileHandle.CreateAnonymousPipe | 8 | Excellent code sample, heading is API-inventory |\n| FileStream async on Unix | 7 | Good but bur