ures items and adjusts scroll estimates has a slightly awkward tone. In fsharp.md, the opening statement about no significant feature changes is passive and negative, there's a confusing double negative with \"no longer inconsistently filters out,\" and the structure puts the \"What's new\" link at the bottom instead of the top where it should be.\n\nThe `Dictionary<T>` error stands out as a factual bug that needs fixing, but the Virtualize code mismatch is probably more impactful for readers trying to follow along. I need to prioritize which one to tackle first based on what will actually help users most. I'm noticing the `Dictionary<T>` notation is problematic—the actual .NET type is `Dictionary<TKey, TValue>`, not `Dictionary<T>`. This appears in both the heading and the body text, and it violates the \"no invented API names\" rule since `Dictionary<T>` doesn't actually exist. I should flag this as a clear issue in my review.\n\nNow I'm moving through the document to compile the full feedback. The user provided the content directly, so I'm working from what they gave me. I'm seeing several violations: the heading \"Runtime Async (V3)\" breaks the 3-8 word guideline and uses non-standard versioning syntax, the intro paragraph has promotional language and passive phrasing that should either be cut or replaced with a single factual statement, and the crossgen2 section has a heading that's too short and uses unexplained jargon.\n\nFor the continuation reuse section, the heading is also below the 3-word minimum. The profiler section is missing context about why the `GetNativeCodeInfo` change matters to readers. The GC regions paragraph makes speculative claims without data, so I should either back it up or simplify it to just note parity with other platforms. And the SDSU interval register allocation heading is too opaque—I need to either clarify it or replace it with something more accessible.\n\nLooking at the libraries.md issues, the title format is inconsistent with runtime.md, so I need to standardize that. The SafeFileHandle section leads with what was added rather than the problem it solves. The Dictionary improvements heading has a type annotation error and uses vague language, so I need to fix both the heading and add concrete details.\n\nNow examining the structure more carefully — FileStream async handles and RandomAccess are listed under SafeFileHandle but they're actually separate features that should either be promoted to their own sections or have their relationship clarified upfront. The Community contributors section is just an empty placeholder, so that either needs actual names or should be removed entirely.\n\nMoving to aspnetcore.md, the Virtualize component section has a code example that doesn't fully demonstrate the feature. The `ItemSize` callback mismatch in the Blazor Virtualize section is probably the most critical issue since developers will try to copy that code directly, but the factual error in `Dictionary<T>` is also a clear violation since it invents an API name that doesn't exist. I'm leaning toward fixing the Dictionary issue first since it's a straightforward factual correction, though the Virtualize confusion could mislead more readers in practice. I'm verifying my concern about the Virtualize example—the prose describes supplying an `ItemSize` callback, but the code shows a standard `<Virtualize>` component with no `ItemSize` at all, which is definitely a mismatch that will confuse readers trying to implement it. Even if the new behavior is automatic, either the code or prose needs fixing. That said, the `Dictionary<T>` naming error is a clear rubric violation and an unambiguous fix, so I'm torn between these two as my final pick.\n\nThe `Dictionary<T>` issue is the safer choice since it's objectively wrong by any standard, whereas the Virtualize mismatch requires knowing the actual API behavior. I'll prioritize the naming error and write it up.",
