    "output": "runtime.md / Runtime Async (V3) / The heading and intro use unexplained jargon (\"Runtime Async\", \"runtime-native async infrastructure\") and promotional phrasing (\"two significant advances\"). Readers do not quickly learn what changed or why to care. / Runtime async adds two changes in Preview 3: crossgen2 can now precompile apps that enable `runtime-async=on`, and the runtime now reuses continuation objects to reduce allocations in async-heavy workloads.\n\nruntime.md / Profiler and diagnostic tool support / \"async thunk methods\" is unexplained jargon, so the user benefit is buried. / Profilers and sampling tools can now attribute time spent in runtime-async methods more accurately because `GetNativeCodeInfo` recognizes the generated helper methods. ([dotnet/runtime #124354](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/124354))\n\nruntime.md / GC regions on macOS / \"Apple silicon workloads in particular may benefit\" is speculative and weakens an otherwise concrete default-on change. / GC regions are now on by default on macOS. This lets the GC reclaim smaller heap segments, which can reduce fragmentation and memory use in long-running apps. ([dotnet/runtime #125416](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125416))\n\nruntime.md / SDSU interval register allocation / The heading and body are compiler-internal jargon for most readers (\"SDSU\", \"intervals\", \"destroyed by nearby calls\", \"spills\"). This is low-value at top level unless rewritten around the user-visible outcome. / Reduce register spills around calls. The JIT now keeps more short-lived values in registers across nearby helper calls, which can shrink generated code in some methods. ([dotnet/runtime #125219](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125219))\n\nlibraries.md / .NET Libraries in .NET 11 Preview 3 - Release Notes / The page opens with repetitive, promotional navigation instead of a clear summary of what changed. / .NET 11 Preview 3 adds new pipe, file, and proxy APIs, plus a small set of performance and bug-fix updates.\n\nlibraries.md / SafeFileHandle.CreateAnonymousPipe / The heading is API-inventory mode instead of a reader-centric title. / Create anonymous pipes with `SafeFileHandle`\n\nlibraries.md / SafeFileHandle.CreateAnonymousPipe / The section leads with signature details instead of the problem it solves. / You can now create an anonymous pipe and control async and non-blocking behavior on each end without platform-specific code or manual P/Invoke. The API works on Windows and Unix. ([dotnet/runtime #125220](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125220))\n\nlibraries.md / FileStream async handles on Unix / This is a separate feature nested under the pipe API, which makes the structure misleading. \"true async I/O semantics\" is also jargon. / Make `FileStream` async handles work on Unix. When you pass a `SafeFileHandle` with `isAsync: true` on Linux or macOS, `FileStream` now performs async I/O instead of falling back to synchronous behavior. ([dotnet/runtime #125377](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125377))\n\nlibraries.md / WebProxy credentials from URI userinfo / \"userinfo\" is RFC jargon, and the heading is API-inventory mode. / Read proxy credentials from the URI. `WebProxy` now uses the username and password embedded in the proxy URI, so you no longer need to set `Credentials` separately. ([dotnet/runtime #125384](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/pull/125384))\n\nlibraries.md / Performance / This section is low-value for most readers: it is compiler-internal, has no numbers, and relies on jargon like \"bounds-check elimination\", \"hot path\", and \"codegen\". / Cut this section from the top-level notes, or group it under a short \"Smaller performance improvements\" note with a benchmark or concrete scenario.\n\nlibraries.md / Community contributors / This is not a product change and distracts from the release notes summary. / Cut from the feature page, or move it to a separate acknowledgments section.\n\naspnetcore.md / Virtualize component supports variable-height items / The example does not match the text. The copy says to supply an `ItemSize` callback, but the sample shows no `ItemSize` at all. / `Virtualize` now supports items with different heights. Before Preview 3, all items had to use the same fixed height. To enable this mode, pass an `ItemSize` callback and show that callback in the sample so readers can see how to use the feature. ([dotnet/aspnetcore #64964](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/64964))\n\naspnetcore.md / Azure SQL with Managed Identity in Individual Auth templates / The heading is long and template/package-centric, and the body leads with a package name instead of the user outcome. / Use Azure SQL managed identity in Individual Auth templates. The template now includes the Azure SQL support needed to use managed identity when you deploy to Azure, so you can avoid storing SQL credentials in configuration. ([dotnet/dotnet #5916](https://github.com/dotnet/dotnet/pull/5916))\n\nfsharp.md / .NET 11 Preview 3 / \"No significant F# feature changes are highlighted\" opens on a negative, low-value note. / This preview focuses on F# compiler, IDE, and tooling fixes.\n\naspnetcore.md / Virtualize component supports variable-height items / Highest-priority rewrite / `Virtualize` now supports items with different heights. Before Preview 3, all items had to use the same fixed height. To enable this mode, pass an `ItemSize` callback and show that callback in the sample. The component then measures rendered items and updates scroll estimates as sizes change. ([dotnet/aspnetcore #64964](https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/pull/64964))"
